AB  - Radar automatic target recognition based on high-resolution range profile (HRRP) has received extensive attention in recent years. Due to its ability to capture fine-grained structural features of targets, the HRRP enables reliable target classification and exhibits well adaptability to deep learning-based recognition frameworks. However, despite the remarkable progress of HRRP recognition techniques, target recognition under electronic countermeasures, especially under the mainstream interrupted-sampling repeater jamming, remains a significant challenge, where the HRRP suffers from serious feature distortion. To overcome this limitation, this article introduces an HRRP recognition framework assisted by prior jamming information. Specifically, we propose a prior knowledge representation method based on the point spread function to model the HRRP distortion induced by jamming. Based on this, the recognition framework incorporates a multiscale feature extraction module designed to thoroughly capture HRRP features, a feature interaction module using a cross-attention mechanism that integrates HRRP features with prior jamming information, and a hybrid loss function that enhances the model’s representation capability. With the aid of prior information, the model can learn invariant features within distorted HRRP under different jamming parameters. Evaluations on both simulated and measured datasets show that the proposed method consistently outperforms state-of-the-art ones in all metrics. Moreover, it exhibits stronger generalization capabilities when facing unseen jamming parameters.
