AB  - With the expansion of the medical and health-care consumer electronics market, Drug-Drug Interaction Event (DDIE) prediction has emerged as a crucial safeguard for identifying potential risks in clinical concomitant drugs to enhance the safety assurance within the health consumer sector. Recently, drug structural representations-based DDIE prediction is the mainstream trend. However, existing methods generally struggle to capture stable molecular representations, resulting in substantial performance fluctuations when the molecular structural distribution shifts. To bridge this gap, we propose the Core-Aware Contrastive Learning with Substructure Adaptivity (CCSA) to enhance the stability of DDIE prediction in distribution-shifted data. CCSA improves the model’s ability to learn stable representations through dynamic substructure adjustment and contrastive learning. Specifically, we introduce a division entropy-supervised substructure adaptive adjustment mechanism to promote diversity in substructure partitioning and optimization. This mechanism facilitates the filtering of irrelevant atomic noise and enriches molecular representations. Additionally, CCSA leverages substructure interactions to distinguish core substructures from confounding (i.e., non-core) substructures. By contrast learning between core substructures, CCSA enhances the consistency and robustness of molecular representations. Experimental results demonstrate that the performance and stability of CCSA outperform seven baseline models. CCSA’s satisfying explainability provides a reliable foundation for its integration and deployment in medical auxiliary electronic devices.
